%!PS-Adobe-3.0
%%Title: drychalk.ps — chalk on a board: the chalk preset and board dressing
%%Creator: pscat — issue #235 (design: docs/DRYMEDIA.md)
%%EndComments
% @requires: (lib/artkit.ps) run (lib/paintkit.ps) run (lib/drykit.ps) run

% Chalk for drykit (epic #232). Three public procedures:
%
%   opts dcchalk -          strokes the current path as chalk
%   /name dcchalkcolor -    sets the current colour to a stick of chalk
%   x y w h opts dcboard -  dresses a board: tone, haze, erased writing,
%                           eraser swipes, wipes and ledge dust
%
% `dcchalk` is a preset over drykit's `dkstroke` (lib/drykit.ps, never
% edited from here). It splits the path into its subpaths, one per pen
% stroke, and gives each stroke what the reference board says real chalk
% does (docs/drymedia/REFERENCES.md, C1-C9):
%
% * Grey, not white (C1). Measured on the cryptography crop, a stroke's
%   core sits about halfway from the board to white, and varies by about
%   a tenth along the stroke. The deposit is three drykit levels at a
%   modest /Strength, so most of a stroke is the middle grey and only the
%   pressed parts reach the top.
% * Landing and lifting (C2, C3). A stroke lands heavy (higher touch,
%   slightly wider), tires in the middle, and lifts thin and faint. The
%   lift tapers the width to a floor, never to a point.
% * Each stroke differs. Every stroke's body touch moves by up to one
%   touch band, its width by up to a tenth, and its strength takes one of
%   three values, from a private generator seeded by the stroke's own
%   position. A word never reads as one stamped weight: measured on the
%   cryptography crop, stroke brightness spreads by about 0.06 of the
%   board-to-white range, and here by 0.05. Touch is quantized to drykit's
%   16 bands and strength to three steps, so strokes still share catches.
% * Dots are pressed (C3): a subpath shorter than about one and a half
%   widths is a dot, drawn wider and harder.
% * Dust (C5, C9): loose specks beside each stroke, drifting down the
%   board, and a narrow halo. A wide halo reads as a grey outline, not
%   dust, so the dusting comes from the specks (`dcdust`, one fill per
%   call, not drykit's /Fallout: see #308).
% * Side of the stick (C3, C6). With /Tilt the stick lies on its side:
%   the mark is drawn as parallel lanes across the stick's contact, each
%   its own drykit stroke. The edge lanes press harder than the worn face
%   between them (two bright edges, a granular middle, C3), and every lane
%   starts and ends a little apart, so a broad mark has a ragged square
%   end rather than the core's three round bumps.
%
% `dcboard` paints the board itself: its tone, a dust haze that is never
% flat, erased writing, eraser swipes, finger wipes and dust along the
% ledge. The haze is one `colorimage` of a smooth field, so it costs one
% sample per few points rather than a fill per cloud, and needs no alpha
% (Ghostscript has none callable from PostScript).
%
% Determinism. Nothing here draws from the caller's `rand` stream:
% drychalk's own generator (`dcrand`) is drykit's Schrage-form LCG under
% its own state, and every random choice is seeded from a position or a
% /Seed. Ghostscript closes sub-pixel gaps in fills (fill adjust), so the
% fine marks drawn here outside drykit (swipe streaks, specks) are drawn
% with the same guarded `.setfilladjust2` drykit uses (DRYMEDIA.md 2.4).
%
% Scratch prefix dc-. Nothing here touches dk-, pk- or pj- names.

% ------------------------------------------------------------- scratch

% The public procedures run with this dictionary on top of the dictionary
% stack, so their working names (and drykit's, defined while they run)
% land here instead of in userdict. Ghostscript's dictionaries are open
% hash tables: userdict, already holding artkit's, paintkit's and
% drykit's names, slows every lookup that misses it (every operator)
% once it is nearly full, and a board's names were enough to double the
% cost of the chalk drawn after it.
%
% Re-measured with every procedure bound (#318): the dictionary still
% pays. Taking it out (userdict in its place) made the specimen run
% 2.7 to 3.1 s in gs against 2.2 s with it, so it stays even though a
% non-userdict dictionary on top makes every unbound lookup dearer
% (notes/307-bind-library-procedures.md).
% @internal
/dcnewscratch { 1200 dict } bind def
% @internal
/dcscratch dcnewscratch def

% ------------------------------------------------------------- generator

% seed -   (seeds drychalk's own generator; drykit's dksrand, own state)
% @internal
/dcsrand {
    cvi 2147483646 mod abs 1 add
    userdict /dcstate 3 -1 roll put
} bind def

% -> 0.0..1.0   (exclusive at both ends; Schrage's form, 32-bit safe)
% @internal
/dcrand {
    userdict /dcstate get
    dup 127773 idiv 2836 mul exch 127773 mod 16807 mul exch sub
    dup 0 lt { 2147483647 add } if
    dup userdict /dcstate 3 -1 roll put
    2147483647 div
} bind def

% @internal
/dcstate 2 def

% ------------------------------------------------------------- helpers

% any -> bool   (an integer or a real)
% @internal
/dcisnum {
    type dup /integertype eq exch /realtype eq or
} bind def

% any -> bool   (an executable array or packed array: a procedure)
% @internal
/dcisproc {
    dup xcheck exch type dup /arraytype eq exch /packedarraytype eq or and
} bind def

% dict key default -> value
% @internal
/dcget {
    /dcgd exch def
    2 copy known { get } { pop pop /dcgd load } ifelse
} bind def

% dict key default lo hi /error -> number   (a number option in lo..hi)
% @internal
/dcoptnum {
    /dcoe exch def /dcoh exch def /dcol exch def
    dcget
    dup dcisnum not { pop dcoe cvx exec } if
    dup dcol lt 1 index dcoh gt or { pop dcoe cvx exec } if
} bind def

% value /error -> [r g b]   (an rgb array of three numbers 0..1)
% @internal
/dcrgb {
    /dcoe exch def
    dup type /arraytype ne { dcoe cvx exec } if
    dup xcheck { dcoe cvx exec } if
    dup length 3 ne { dcoe cvx exec } if
    dup {
        dup dcisnum not { pop dcoe cvx exec } if
        dup 0 lt exch 1 gt or { dcoe cvx exec } if
    } forall
    aload pop 3 array astore
} bind def

% mark x1 .. xn -> [x1 .. xn]
% @internal
/dctomark {
    counttomark array astore exch pop
} bind def

% a b t -> a + (b - a) t   (numbers)
% @internal
/dclerp {
    /dclt exch def 1 index sub dclt mul add
} bind def

% [a] [b] t -> [a + (b - a) t]   (rgb arrays)
% @internal
/dclerp3 {
    /dclu exch def /dclb exch def /dcla exch def
    [ 0 1 2 { /dclk exch def
        dcla dclk get dclb dclk get dcla dclk get sub dclu mul add } for ]
} bind def

% x y -> seed   (a user-space point's canvas position, to 0.01pt, folded
% into a 31-bit seed: the same point gives the same seed in any CTM)
% @internal
/dcposseed {
    transform userdict /pjCTM get itransform
    100 mul round dup abs 2e9 gt { pop 0 } if cvi 46337 mod abs
    exch
    100 mul round dup abs 2e9 gt { pop 0 } if cvi 46337 mod abs
    46337 mul add
} bind def

% - -> [ [x0 y0 x1 y1 ...] ... ]   (the current path, flattened, one
% point array per subpath; a closed subpath repeats its first point)
% @internal
/dcsubpaths {
    flattenpath
    /dcSopen false def
    mark
    {
        /dcmy exch def /dcmx exch def
        dcSopen { dctomark } if
        mark dcmx dcmy
        /dcSopen true def
    }
    {
        dcSopen not {
            /dcmy exch def /dcmx exch def mark dcmx dcmy /dcSopen true def
        } if
    }
    { 6 { pop } repeat }
    {
        dcSopen {
            counttomark 1 sub index counttomark 2 sub index
            dctomark
            /dcSopen false def
        } if
    }
    pathforall
    dcSopen { dctomark } if
    dctomark
    newpath
} bind def

% [x0 y0 ...] -> length   (the polyline's length)
% @internal
/dcplen {
    /dcpp exch def
    0
    2 2 dcpp length 2 sub {
        /dcpi exch def
        dcpp dcpi get dcpp dcpi 2 sub get sub dup mul
        dcpp dcpi 1 add get dcpp dcpi 1 sub get sub dup mul add sqrt add
    } for
} bind def

% [x0 y0 ...] -   (the polyline as a new path)
% @internal
/dcpolypath {
    /dcpp exch def
    newpath
    dcpp 0 get dcpp 1 get moveto
    dcpp length 2 gt {
        2 2 dcpp length 2 sub { dup dcpp exch get exch 1 add dcpp exch get lineto } for
    } {
        % a single point: a zero-length segment, so drykit sees a dot
        dcpp 0 get dcpp 1 get lineto
    } ifelse
} bind def

% ----------------------------------------------------------------- chalk

% The touch quantum: drykit quantizes a /Touch procedure to /Bands levels,
% and chalk always uses 16, so one band is 1/16.
% @internal
/dcband 0.0625 def

% Chalk colours: white, and soft colours as real coloured chalk reads on
% a board (pale, chalky, never saturated). The pigment is drawn at
% /Strength against the board, so these are the stick's own colour.
% @internal
/dcchalks <<
    /white  [0.95 0.95 0.92]
    /yellow [0.99 0.91 0.45]
    /pink   [0.98 0.62 0.74]
    /blue   [0.55 0.78 0.99]
    /green  [0.66 0.92 0.58]
    /orange [1.00 0.70 0.40]
>> def

% t -> touch   (the stroke's touch profile, from the dcT* globals that
% dcchalk sets for the stroke being recorded: a heavy landing, a tired
% middle, a fading lift)
% @internal
/dctouchat {
    /dctt exch def
    dcTb
    dctt dcTlandL lt { dcTland add } if
    dctt 0.3 gt dctt 0.75 lt and { dcband 0.5 mul sub } if
    dctt 0.75 ge { dctt 0.75 sub 0.25 div dcTlift mul sub } if
    dup 0.02 lt { pop 0.02 } if dup 1 gt { pop 1 } if
} bind def

% t -> width multiplier   (landing slightly wide, the lift thinning to a
% floor, so a stroke never ends in a point)
% @internal
/dcpressat {
    /dcpt exch def
    dcpt dcTlandL lt {
        1 dcTland 1.6 mul add
    } {
        dcpt 0.7 gt {
            dcpt 0.7 sub 0.3 div dup sqrt mul
            1 dcTfloor sub mul 1 exch sub
        } { 1 } ifelse
    } ifelse
} bind def

% pts -> pts'   (a dot as a short dab: the stick presses and drags a
% little, so a dot is never a clean disc. Centred on the dot's points,
% half a width long, along the dot's own direction or a seeded one.)
% @internal
/dcdotpath {
    /dcdp exch def
    /dcdx dcdp 0 get def /dcdy dcdp 1 get def
    /dcdex dcdp dcdp length 2 sub get def /dcdey dcdp dcdp length 1 sub get def
    /dcdcx dcdx dcdex add 2 div def /dcdcy dcdy dcdey add 2 div def
    dcdex dcdx sub abs dcdey dcdy sub abs add 1e-6 gt {
        dcdey dcdy sub dcdex dcdx sub atan
    } {
        dcdx dcdy dcposseed dcsrand dcrand 360 mul
    } ifelse
    /dcda exch def
    /dcdl dcw 0.25 mul def
    [ dcdcx dcda cos dcdl mul sub dcdcy dcda sin dcdl mul sub
      dcdcx dcda cos dcdl mul add dcdcy dcda sin dcdl mul add ]
} bind def

% pts -   (one subpath as one chalk stroke with the stick's tip)
% @internal
/dctipstroke {
    /dcpts exch def
    /dcL dcpts dcplen def
    /dcdot dcL dcw 1.5 mul lt def
    dcdot { /dcpts dcpts dcdotpath def /dcL dcpts dcplen def } if
    % the stroke's own variation: body touch moves by a band either way
    dcpts 0 get dcpts 1 get dcposseed dcSeedOn { pop dcSeed dcSi 7919 mul add } if
    dcsrand dcrand pop
    /dcr1 dcrand def /dcr2 dcrand def /dcr3 dcrand def
    /dcTb dcTouch dcr1 dcVary mul 0.5 dcVary mul sub 2 mul dcband mul add def
    /dcTland dcLanding def
    /dcTlandL dcdot { 2 } { 2.4 dcw mul dcL 1e-6 dcmax div 0.06 dcmax 0.3 dcmin } ifelse def
    /dcTlift dcLift dcband 2.5 mul mul def
    /dcTfloor 1 dcLift 0.55 mul sub def
    % a dot is pressed: half the landing's extra touch for the whole dab
    % (all of it reads as a clean disc), Codex review of PR #310
    dcdot { /dcTb dcTb dcLanding 0.5 mul add def /dcTland 0 def } if
    /dcww dcw 1 dcr2 0.5 sub dcVary mul 0.24 mul add mul
        dcdot { 1.2 mul } if def
    dcSo /Width dcww put
    % brightness differs from stroke to stroke as much as within one (C1:
    % peaks on the photographed board spread by about a twelfth): one of
    % three strengths, so strokes still share catches
    dcSo /Strength dcStrength
        dcr3 0.5 sub dcVary mul 3 mul round -1 dcmax 1 dcmin 0.15 mul 1 add mul
        1 dcmin put
    dcSo /Touch { dctouchat } put
    dcSo /Pressure { dcpressat } put
    dcSeedOn { dcSo /Seed dcSeed dcSi 7919 mul add 2147483 mod put } if
    dcpts dcpolypath
    dcSo dkstroke
} bind def

% a b -> max, min
% @internal
/dcmax { 2 copy lt { exch } if pop } bind def
% @internal
/dcmin { 2 copy gt { exch } if pop } bind def

% pts -   (one subpath as a broad side-of-chalk mark: parallel lanes)
% @internal
/dcsidestroke {
    /dcpts exch def
    /dcn dcpts length 2 idiv def
    % normals at each vertex, from the neighbouring segments
    /dcnx dcn array def /dcny dcn array def
    0 1 dcn 1 sub {
        /dcvi exch def
        /dcva dcvi 1 sub 0 dcmax def /dcvb dcvi 1 add dcn 1 sub dcmin def
        dcpts dcvb 2 mul get dcpts dcva 2 mul get sub
        dcpts dcvb 2 mul 1 add get dcpts dcva 2 mul 1 add get sub
        2 copy dup mul exch dup mul add sqrt dup 1e-9 lt { pop pop pop 1 0 1 } if
        /dcvl exch def /dcvy exch dcvl div def /dcvx exch dcvl div def
        dcnx dcvi dcvy neg put dcny dcvi dcvx put
    } for
    /dcL dcpts dcplen def
    /dccw dcw 1 dcTiltS sub mul dcSide dcTiltS mul add def
    /dck dccw dcw 1.4 mul div round cvi 3 dcmax 8 dcmin def
    /dcew dccw dck div 0.9 mul def
    /dcfh dccw 2 div dcew 0.6 mul sub def
    /dcfs dcfh 2 mul dck 2 sub div def
    dcpts 0 get dcpts 1 get dcposseed dcSeedOn { pop dcSeed dcSi 7919 mul add } if
    dcsrand dcrand pop
    0 1 dck 1 sub {
        /dcli exch def
        /dcedge dcli 0 eq dcli dck 1 sub eq or def
        % the two edge lanes are narrow and sit at the patch's rims; the
        % face lanes share what lies between, overlapping
        dcedge {
            /dclw dcew def
            /dclo dccw dcew sub 2 div dcli 0 eq { neg } if def
        } {
            /dclw dcfs 1.6 mul def
            /dclo dcli 1 sub 0.5 add dcfs mul dcfh sub def
        } ifelse
        % each lane lands and lifts a little apart: a ragged square end
        /dcs0 dcrand dccw dck div 1.2 mul mul def
        /dcs1 dcrand dccw dck div 1.2 mul mul def
        [
            0 1 dcn 1 sub {
                /dcvi exch def
                dcpts dcvi 2 mul get dcnx dcvi get dclo mul add
                dcpts dcvi 2 mul 1 add get dcny dcvi get dclo mul add
            } for
        ] /dclp exch def
        % trim the lane's two ends along the path
        dcL dcs0 dcs1 add 2 add gt {
            dclp dcs0 dcs1 dctrim /dclp exch def
        } if
        /dcTb dcTouch dcedge { dcband add } { dcband 2 mul sub } ifelse
            dcrand 0.5 sub dcVary mul 2 mul dcband mul add def
        /dcTland dcLanding 0.5 mul def
        /dcTlandL 0.08 def
        /dcTlift dcLift dcband 2 mul mul def
        /dcTfloor 1 dcLift 0.3 mul sub def
        dcSo /Width dclw put
        dcSo /Touch { dctouchat } put
        dcSo /Pressure { dcpressat } put
        dcSo /Streak dcStreak 0.6 add 4 dcmin put
        dcSo /Dropout dcedge { 0 } { 0.012 } ifelse put
        dcSo /Load 0.8 put
        dcSeedOn { dcSo /Seed dcSeed dcSi 7919 mul add dcli 31 mul add 2147483 mod put } if
        dclp dcpolypath
        dcSo dkstroke
    } for
    dcSo /Streak dcStreak put
    dcSo /Dropout 0 put
    dcSo /Load 1 put
} bind def

% pts s0 s1 -> pts'   (the polyline with s0 points cut from its start and
% s1 from its end, measured along it)
% @internal
/dctrim {
    /dct1 exch def /dct0 exch def /dctp exch def
    /dctn dctp length 2 idiv def
    /dctL dctp dcplen def
    /dcta dct0 def /dctb dctL dct1 sub def
    mark
    /dcts 0 def
    0 1 dctn 2 sub {
        /dcti exch def
        /dctx0 dctp dcti 2 mul get def /dcty0 dctp dcti 2 mul 1 add get def
        /dctx1 dctp dcti 2 mul 2 add get def /dcty1 dctp dcti 2 mul 3 add get def
        /dctl dctx1 dctx0 sub dup mul dcty1 dcty0 sub dup mul add sqrt def
        /dcte dcts dctl add def
        dctl 0 gt {
            % the segment's part inside [a, b]
            dcta dcts dcmax /dctu0 exch def
            dctb dcte dcmin /dctu1 exch def
            dctu0 dctu1 lt {
                counttomark 0 eq {
                    dctx0 dctx1 dctx0 sub dctu0 dcts sub dctl div mul add
                    dcty0 dcty1 dcty0 sub dctu0 dcts sub dctl div mul add
                } if
                dctx0 dctx1 dctx0 sub dctu1 dcts sub dctl div mul add
                dcty0 dcty1 dcty0 sub dctu1 dcts sub dctl div mul add
            } if
        } if
        /dcts dcte def
    } for
    counttomark 4 lt { cleartomark dctp } { dctomark } ifelse
} bind def

% opts -   (validates dcchalk's keys into dc* globals)
% @internal
/dcchalkopts {
    /dcO exch def
    /dcO load type /dicttype ne { dcchalk-options-must-be-a-dict } if
    userdict /pjCTM known not { dcchalk-needs-a-surface } if
    /dcw dcO /Width 2 1e-6 1e6 /dcchalk-width-must-be-positive dcoptnum def
    /dcTouch dcO /Touch 0.68 0 1 /dcchalk-touch-must-be-0-to-1 dcoptnum def
    /dcLanding dcO /Landing 0.5 0 1 /dcchalk-landing-must-be-0-to-1 dcoptnum def
    /dcLanding dcLanding 3 mul dcband mul def
    /dcLift dcO /Lift 0.5 0 1 /dcchalk-lift-must-be-0-to-1 dcoptnum def
    /dcVary dcO /Vary 0.5 0 1 /dcchalk-vary-must-be-0-to-1 dcoptnum def
    /dcDust dcO /Dust 0.5 0 1 /dcchalk-dust-must-be-0-to-1 dcoptnum def
    /dcTilt dcO /Tilt 0 0 90 /dcchalk-tilt-must-be-0-to-90 dcoptnum def
    /dcSide dcO /Side 10 1e-6 1e6 /dcchalk-side-must-be-positive dcoptnum def
    /dcStrength dcO /Strength 0.55 0 1 /dcchalk-strength-must-be-0-to-1 dcoptnum def
    /dcStreak dcO /Streak 1.6 1 4 /dcchalk-streak-must-be-1-to-4 dcoptnum def
    dcO /Under known {
        dcO /Under get /dcchalk-under-must-be-an-rgb-array dcrgb
    } {
        userdict /dkGround known not { dcchalk-needs-a-surface } if
        % dcboard's tone holds only while its surface does: dksurface
        % writes a fresh /dkGround array, so a later surface (identity, not
        % value) falls back to its own ground (Codex review of PR #310)
        userdict /dcUnder known {
            userdict /dcUnderG get userdict /dkGround get eq
        } { false } ifelse
        { userdict /dcUnder get } { userdict /dkGround get } ifelse
    } ifelse
    /dcUnderC exch def
    dcO /Seed known {
        dcO /Seed get dup dcisnum not { pop dcchalk-seed-must-be-a-number } if
        abs 2147483647 ge { dcchalk-seed-must-be-within-integer-range } if
        /dcSeedOn true def /dcSeed dcO /Seed get cvi 2147483 mod def
    } { /dcSeedOn false def } ifelse
    /dcTiltS dcTilt sin def
} bind def

% -   (the loose dust of every stroke in dcsubs: specks beside the line,
% thinning away from it and drifting down the board, all in one fill.
% Drawn by hand rather than through drykit's /Fallout, whose per-piece
% `scatter` calls cost Ghostscript more than the strokes themselves.)
% @internal
/dcdust {
    dcDust 0 gt {
        gsave
            /.setfilladjust2 where { pop 0 0 .setfilladjust2 } if
            dcUnderC [ currentrgbcolor ] dcStrength 0.6 mul dclerp3 aload pop setrgbcolor
            % the drift: down the board, in this user space
            0 dcw dcDust mul 3 mul neg userdict /pjCTM get dtransform idtransform
            /dcdry exch def /dcdrx exch def
            /dccw dcw 1 dcTiltS sub mul dcSide dcTiltS mul add def
            newpath
            0 1 dcsubs length 1 sub {
                /dcSi exch def
                /dcpts dcsubs dcSi get def
                dcpts 0 get dcpts 1 get dcposseed 104729 add
                    dcSeedOn { pop dcSeed dcSi 7919 mul add 13 add } if
                dcsrand dcrand pop
                0 2 dcpts length 4 sub {
                    /dcvi exch def
                    /dcax dcpts dcvi get def /dcay dcpts dcvi 1 add get def
                    /dcbx dcpts dcvi 2 add get dcax sub def
                    /dcby dcpts dcvi 3 add get dcay sub def
                    /dcsl dcbx dup mul dcby dup mul add sqrt def
                    dcsl 0 gt {
                        dcsl dcDust mul 0.7 mul dcrand add floor cvi {
                            /dcst dcrand def
                            % across the line: most specks close to it
                            /dcso dcrand 0.5 sub dup abs mul 4 mul 0.5 add dccw mul def
                            dcrand 0.5 lt { /dcso dcso neg def } if
                            /dcsd dcrand dup mul def
                            dcax dcbx dcst mul add dcby neg dcsl div dcso mul add dcdrx dcsd mul add
                            dcay dcby dcst mul add dcbx dcsl div dcso mul add dcdry dcsd mul add
                            /dcpy exch def /dcpx exch def
                            /dcpr dcrand 0.18 mul 0.1 add def
                            dcpx dcpr add dcpy moveto
                            dcpx dcpy dcpr 0 360 arc closepath
                        } repeat
                    } if
                } for
            } for
            fill
        grestore
    } if
} bind def

% -   (records every subpath of the current path; dcchalk's body)
% @internal
/dcchalkbody {
    /dcSo 24 dict def
    dcSo /Levels dcLevels put
    dcSo /Bands 16 put
    dcSo /Strength dcStrength put
    dcSo /Under dcUnderC put
    dcSo /Streak dcStreak put
    dcSo /Halo dcDust 0.1 mul put
    /dcsubs dcsubpaths def
    0 1 dcsubs length 1 sub {
        /dcSi exch def
        dcsubs dcSi get
        dcTilt 0 gt { dcsidestroke } { dctipstroke } ifelse
    } for
} bind def

%%SelfTest: dcchalk-rejects-malformed-options
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%   { << >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-needs-a-surface (chalk before any surface) mustguard
%   << /Surface /slate >> dksurface
%   newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto
%   { 5 dcchalk } /dcchalk-options-must-be-a-dict (a number for the options) mustguard
%   { << /Width 0 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-width-must-be-positive (/Width 0) mustguard
%   { << /Touch 1.5 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-touch-must-be-0-to-1 (/Touch 1.5) mustguard
%   { << /Landing -1 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-landing-must-be-0-to-1 (/Landing -1) mustguard
%   { << /Lift 2 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-lift-must-be-0-to-1 (/Lift 2) mustguard
%   { << /Vary (x) >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-vary-must-be-0-to-1 (a string /Vary) mustguard
%   { << /Dust 3 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-dust-must-be-0-to-1 (/Dust 3) mustguard
%   { << /Tilt 100 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-tilt-must-be-0-to-90 (/Tilt 100) mustguard
%   { << /Side 0 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-side-must-be-positive (/Side 0) mustguard
%   { << /Strength -0.1 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-strength-must-be-0-to-1 (/Strength -0.1) mustguard
%   { << /Streak 9 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-streak-must-be-1-to-4 (/Streak 9) mustguard
%   { << /Under [1 1] >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-under-must-be-an-rgb-array (a two-entry /Under) mustguard
%   { << /Seed (s) >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-seed-must-be-a-number (a string /Seed) mustguard
%   { << /Seed 1e12 >> dcchalk } /dcchalk-seed-must-be-within-integer-range (/Seed 1e12) mustguard
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << >> dcchalk grestore } (the defaults) mustpass
%   { gsave newpath 0 0 moveto 50 0 lineto << /Tilt 80 >> dcchalk grestore } (a side stroke) mustpass
%   { gsave newpath 5 5 moveto << >> dcchalk grestore } (a bare moveto) mustpass
%   { gsave newpath << >> dcchalk grestore } (an empty path) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: dcchalk-takes-no-draws-from-the-callers-stream
%   << /Surface /greenboard >> dksurface
%   1 srand rand /sttaken exch def
%   1 srand
%   gsave newpath 10 10 moveto 90 40 lineto 30 80 moveto 31 80 lineto
%   << /Width 3 /Dust 1 >> dcchalk
%   newpath 10 10 moveto 90 40 lineto << /Tilt 85 /Side 8 >> dcchalk grestore
%   rand sttaken eq
%   (dcchalk, tip and side, leaves the caller's stream untouched) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Strokes the current path as chalk on a board: each subpath is one stroke of the stick, drawn through drykit's dkstroke on the surface set by dksurface or dcboard, in the current colour. Every stroke lands heavy, tires in the middle and lifts thin and faint; its weight differs a little from its neighbours'; dots are pressed harder; dust falls beside it and drifts down the board. With /Tilt the stick lies on its side for broad underlines and shading, two bright edges over a granular face. Consumes the current path; takes no rand draws from the caller's stream.
% @example: << /Surface /greenboard >> dksurface /white dcchalkcolor newpath 40 60 moveto 300 80 lineto << /Width 2.4 >> dcchalk
% @param: /Width [points] Width of the stick's tip; must be > 0 (default 2)
% @param: /Touch [0..1] Pressure of the stroke's body against the board's tooth: low breaks into specks, 1 is solid (default 0.68)
% @param: /Landing [0..1] How much harder and wider the stroke lands than its body, and how much harder a dot is pressed (default 0.5)
% @param: /Lift [0..1] How far the stroke fades and thins where the chalk lifts; the width tapers to a floor, never a point (default 0.5)
% @param: /Vary [0..1] How much each stroke's weight and width differ from the next one's, so no two strokes read stamped (default 0.5)
% @param: /Dust [0..1] Loose chalk dust beside each stroke, falling down the board, plus a narrow halo (default 0.5)
% @param: /Tilt [degrees] 0 writes with the tip; toward 90 the stick lies on its side and the mark widens toward /Side as parallel lanes, the edges pressing harder than the face (default 0)
% @param: /Side [points] Length of the stick in contact when it lies on its side at /Tilt 90; must be > 0 (default 10)
% @param: /Strength [0..1] How far the brightest deposit reads from the board toward the chalk's colour; measured on a photographed board, real chalk's brightest strokes sit about halfway (default 0.55)
% @param: /Streak [ratio] How much each tooth knuckle is stretched along the stroke, 1..4: skipped valleys streak with the travel (default 1.6)
% @param: /Under [rgb array] What lies beneath (default the board tone dcboard set, else the dksurface ground)
% @param: /Seed [integer] Seed for every stroke's variation and texture; subpath i uses Seed + 7919 i. Leaves the caller's rand stream untouched (default from each stroke's position on the board)
/dcchalk {
    dcscratch begin
    {
        dcchalkopts
        userdict /dkBatch known {
            dcchalkbody
            dcdust
        } {
            { dcchalkbody } dkbatch
            dcdust
        } ifelse
    } stopped
    end
    { stop } if
} bind def

%%SelfTest: dcchalkcolor-sets-the-colour
%   { /mauve dcchalkcolor } /dcchalkcolor-unknown-colour (/mauve) mustguard
%   { (white) dcchalkcolor } /dcchalkcolor-needs-a-name (a string) mustguard
%   /yellow dcchalkcolor currentrgbcolor pop pop 0.98 gt
%   (/yellow sets its red channel) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Sets the current colour to a stick of chalk: /white, or the soft colours /yellow, /pink, /blue, /green and /orange. dcchalk draws it at /Strength against the board, so the marks read paler and greyer than the stick.
% @example: /yellow dcchalkcolor
/dcchalkcolor {
    dup type /nametype ne { pop dcchalkcolor-needs-a-name } if
    dcchalks 1 index known not { pop dcchalkcolor-unknown-colour } if
    dcchalks exch get aload pop setrgbcolor
} bind def

% ----------------------------------------------------------------- board

% angle -> 0..360   (`mod` takes integers only)
% @internal
/dcdeg {
    dup 360 div floor 360 mul sub
} bind def

% The ghost writing's chalk: wider than the writing (the eraser smeared
% it) and at a light touch (the eraser took the peaks).
% @internal
/dcGhostW 2.4 def
% @internal
/dcGhostT 0.4 def
% Deposit levels of a chalk stroke: three, so most of a stroke is a middle
% grey and only the pressed parts reach the top (the ghost uses one).
% @internal
/dcLevels 3 def

% The board tones (measured against the reference crops: the photographed
% boards read 0.23-0.30 grey, but that includes their dust; the bare tone
% here sits a little darker, and dcboard's haze brings it up).
% @internal
/dcboards <<
    /slate      << /Ground [0.19 0.20 0.22] >>
    /greenboard << /Ground [0.16 0.27 0.21] >>
>> def

% How far toward white a film of 1 reads: dchazek + dchazeq Haze^2. A
% lightly dusted board is still a dark board; a heavily erased one goes
% the milky grey-green of the eraser-swipe crop (C6).
% @internal
/dchazek 0.12 def
% @internal
/dchazeq 0.35 def

% The haze field's sample pitch, in device pixels (3 points at 2x). The
% field is smooth: a block of this size differs from its neighbour by
% about one level of 255, invisible at normal contrast, and the whole
% board is one `colorimage` rather than a fill per cloud.
% @internal
/dcpitch 6 def

% i j -> 0..1   (a hash of lattice point (i, j) through the board's own
% permutation table, dcP)
% @internal
/dchash {
    exch 255 and dcP exch get add 255 and dcP exch get 255 div
} bind def

% u v -> 0..1   (value noise: smoothstepped bilinear between lattice
% hashes at the integer points around (u, v))
% @internal
/dcnoise {
    /dcnv exch def /dcnu exch def
    /dcni dcnu floor def /dcnj dcnv floor def
    /dcnfu dcnu dcni sub def /dcnfv dcnv dcnj sub def
    /dcnfu dcnfu dup mul 3 2 dcnfu mul sub mul def
    /dcnfv dcnfv dup mul 3 2 dcnfv mul sub mul def
    /dcni dcni cvi def /dcnj dcnj cvi def
    dcni dcnj dchash dcni 1 add dcnj dchash dcnfu dclerp
    dcni dcnj 1 add dchash dcni 1 add dcnj 1 add dchash dcnfu dclerp
    dcnfv dclerp
} bind def

% x y -> 0..1   (the dust film at board point (x, y), board-local points)
% @internal
/dcfield {
    /dcfy exch def /dcfx exch def
    % clouds: two octaves of value noise, a large slow one and a finer one
    dcfx dcCs1 mul dcfy dcCs1 mul dcnoise 0.65 mul
    dcfx dcCs2 mul 17.3 add dcfy dcCs2 mul 5.1 add dcnoise 0.35 mul add
    dup mul 1.6 mul dcHaze mul 0.25 dcHaze mul add
    dcHaze dup dup mul mul 0.6 mul add
    % eraser swipes: bands of dust along arcs
    dcSw { dup dcinbox not { pop } {
        0 6 getinterval aload pop /dcsb exch def /dcsa1 exch def /dcsa0 exch def
        /dcsr exch def /dcscy exch def /dcscx exch def
        /dcsdx dcfx dcscx sub def /dcsdy dcfy dcscy sub def
        dcsdx dup mul dcsdy dup mul add sqrt dcsr sub abs dcsb div
        dup 1 lt {
            1 exch sub
            dcsdy dcsdx atan dcsa0 sub dcdeg
            dcsa1 dcsa0 sub dcdeg 2 copy lt {
                % fade over the last fifth of the arc at either end
                div dup 0.2 lt { 0.2 div } { dup 0.8 gt { 1 exch sub 0.2 div } { pop 1 } ifelse } ifelse
                mul 0.55 dcHaze 0.25 mul sub mul add
            } { pop pop pop } ifelse
        } { pop } ifelse
    } ifelse } forall
    % smudges: lighter, elongated smears of dust
    dcSm { dup dcinbox not { pop } {
        0 6 getinterval aload pop /dcmw exch def /dcma exch def /dcmh exch def /dcml exch def
        /dcmy exch def /dcmx exch def
        dcfx dcmx sub dcfy dcmy sub
        2 copy dcma cos mul exch dcma sin mul exch sub dcmh div dup mul
        3 1 roll dcma sin mul exch dcma cos mul add dcml div dup mul add
        dup 1 lt { 1 exch sub dup mul dcmw mul add } { pop } ifelse
    } ifelse } forall
    % the ledge: dust settles thickest along the bottom edge
    dcLedge 0 gt dcfy dcLedge 5 mul lt and {
        dcfy dcLedge div neg 2.718 exch exp 1.6 mul
        dcfx dcCs2 2 mul mul 3.7 add 0.5 dcnoise 0.6 mul 0.7 add mul add
    } if
    % wipes: the film is wiped off, darker than the haze around them
    dcWp { dup dcinbox not { pop } {
        0 6 getinterval aload pop /dcmw exch def /dcma exch def /dcmh exch def /dcml exch def
        /dcmy exch def /dcmx exch def
        dcfx dcmx sub dcfy dcmy sub
        2 copy dcma cos mul exch dcma sin mul exch sub dcmh div dup mul
        3 1 roll dcma sin mul exch dcma cos mul add dcml div dup mul add
        dup 1 lt { sqrt 0.85 mul 0.15 add mul } { pop } ifelse
    } ifelse } forall
    % the film tops out at 2: dust heaped on the ledge reads brighter than
    % any haze
    dup 0 lt { pop 0 } if dup 2 gt { pop 2 } if
} bind def

% f -> r g b   (the board colour under a dust film f)
% @internal
/dcfcolor {
    dchk mul /dcfk exch def
    0 1 2 { dup dcG exch get exch dcWhite exch get dcfk dclerp } for
} bind def

% -   (paints the haze field over the board rectangle, one colorimage)
% @internal
/dcpaintfield {
    gsave
        dcBx dcBy translate
        dcBw dcBh scale
        dcNx dcNy 8 [dcNx 0 0 dcNy 0 0]
        /dcri 0 def
        { dcRows dcri get /dcri dcri 1 add def }
        false 3 colorimage
    grestore
} bind def

% -   (computes the field's rows: dcRows, one rgb string per sample row,
% bottom row first, plus dcFmean, the field's mean)
% @internal
/dcbuildfield {
    % about dcpitch device pixels a sample, 1 to 8 points
    1 0 dtransform dup mul exch dup mul add sqrt
    0 1 dtransform dup mul exch dup mul add sqrt dcmax
    dcpitch exch div 1 dcmax 8 dcmin /dcstep exch def
    /dcNx dcBw dcstep div ceiling cvi 1 dcmax def
    /dcNy dcBh dcstep div ceiling cvi 1 dcmax def
    /dcsx dcBw dcNx div def /dcsy dcBh dcNy div def
    % the field itself is evaluated on a coarse grid, dcgrid samples a
    % cell, and bilinear between: every feature is many cells wide
    % about dcgridpt points a cell whatever the resolution, so a wipe or the
    % ledge's falloff is never narrower than a cell
    /dcgrid dcgridpt dcstep div round cvi 1 dcmax def
    /dcMx dcNx dcgrid div ceiling cvi 1 dcmax def
    /dcMy dcNy dcgrid div ceiling cvi 1 dcmax def
    /dcGx dcBw dcMx div def /dcGy dcBh dcMy div def
    /dcGrid [
        0 1 dcMy {
            dcGy mul /dcry exch def
            [ 0 1 dcMx { dcGx mul dcry dcfield } for ]
        } for
    ] def
    % per column: its coarse cell and the fraction across it
    /dcCi dcNx array def /dcCf dcNx array def
    0 1 dcNx 1 sub {
        /dcrx exch def
        dcrx 0.5 add dcsx mul dcGx div
        dup floor cvi dcMx 1 sub dcmin /dcci exch def
        dcCi dcrx dcci put
        dcci sub dcCf dcrx 3 -1 roll put
    } for
    % bytes are g + d f per channel
    /dcbg [ 0 1 2 { dcG exch get 255 mul } for ] def
    /dcbd [ 0 1 2 { dup dcWhite exch get exch dcG exch get sub dchk mul 255 mul } for ] def
    /dcbg0 dcbg 0 get def /dcbg1 dcbg 1 get def /dcbg2 dcbg 2 get def
    /dcbd0 dcbd 0 get def /dcbd1 dcbd 1 get def /dcbd2 dcbd 2 get def
    /dcFsum 0 def
    /dcRows [
        0 1 dcNy 1 sub {
            0.5 add dcsy mul dcGy div
            dup floor cvi dcMy 1 sub dcmin /dccj exch def
            dccj sub /dcfv exch def
            /dclo dcGrid dccj get def /dchi dcGrid dccj 1 add get def
            /dcrs dcNx 3 mul string def
            0 1 dcNx 1 sub {
                /dcrx exch def
                /dcci dcCi dcrx get def /dcfu dcCf dcrx get def
                dclo dcci get dup dclo dcci 1 add get exch sub dcfu mul add
                dchi dcci get dup dchi dcci 1 add get exch sub dcfu mul add
                1 index sub dcfv mul add
                dup dcFsum add /dcFsum exch def
                /dcff exch def
                dcrs dcrx 3 mul dcbg0 dcbd0 dcff mul add round cvi put
                dcrs dcrx 3 mul 1 add dcbg1 dcbd1 dcff mul add round cvi put
                dcrs dcrx 3 mul 2 add dcbg2 dcbd2 dcff mul add round cvi put
            } for
            dcrs
        } for
    ] def
    /dcFmean dcFsum dcNx dcNy mul div def
} bind def

% The coarse grid the field is evaluated on, in points per cell: the
% field is bilinear between grid points, and every feature (a wipe's
% half-height, the ledge's falloff) is wider than this.
% @internal
/dcgridpt 6 def

% -   (fine streaks along every swipe's arc: the felt's fibres leave lines
% a little lighter or darker than the band they lie in)
% @internal
/dcstreaks {
    gsave
        /.setfilladjust2 where { pop 0 0 .setfilladjust2 } if
        dcBx dcBy translate
        0 setlinecap
        dcSw {
            0 6 getinterval aload pop /dcsb exch def /dcsa1 exch def /dcsa0 exch def
            /dcsr exch def /dcscy exch def /dcscx exch def
            /dcspan dcsa1 dcsa0 sub dcdeg def
            dcsb 1.2 mul cvi 4 dcmax {
                /dcso dcrand 2 mul 1 sub dcsb mul 0.85 mul def
                /dcsf0 dcrand 0.35 mul def
                /dcsf1 dcsf0 0.25 add dcrand 0.4 mul add 1 dcmin def
                % the streak's tone: the band's own, a little lighter or darker
                dcscx dcsr dcso add dcsa0 dcsf0 dcsf1 add 2 div dcspan mul add
                    2 copy cos mul 3 1 roll sin mul dcscy add exch 3 -1 roll add exch
                dcfield dcrand 0.5 sub 0.25 mul add dcfcolor setrgbcolor
                dcrand 0.3 mul 0.15 add setlinewidth
                newpath
                dcscx dcscy dcsr dcso add 0.5 dcmax
                dcsa0 dcsf0 dcspan mul add dcsa0 dcsf1 dcspan mul add arc
                stroke
            } repeat
        } forall
    grestore
} bind def

% -   (the film's grain: dust caught in the board's own texture reads as
% fine specks, a little lighter, more of them where the film is thicker,
% and a few darker pits where the surface is bare. Two fills.)
% @internal
/dcgrain {
    gsave
        /.setfilladjust2 where { pop 0 0 .setfilladjust2 } if
        dcBx dcBy translate
        /dcgn dcBw dcBh mul dcgrainK mul dcHaze 0.3 add mul cvi def
        dcFmean 0.6 add dcfcolor setrgbcolor
        newpath
        dcgn {
            /dcpx dcrand dcBw mul def /dcpy dcrand dcBh mul def
            dcrand dcpx dcpy dcfieldat 0.8 mul 0.15 add lt {
                dcpx dcpy dcrand 0.22 mul 0.12 add dcgdot
            } if
        } repeat
        fill
        dcFmean 0.25 mul dcfcolor setrgbcolor
        newpath
        dcgn 4 idiv {
            dcrand dcBw mul dcrand dcBh mul dcrand 0.18 mul 0.12 add dcgdot
        } repeat
        fill
    grestore
} bind def

% x y -> f   (the film at a board point, from the coarse grid)
% @internal
/dcfieldat {
    dcGy div 0 dcmax dup floor cvi dcMy 1 sub dcmin /dcaj exch def dcaj sub 1 dcmin /dcav exch def
    dcGx div 0 dcmax dup floor cvi dcMx 1 sub dcmin /dcai exch def dcai sub 1 dcmin /dcau exch def
    dcGrid dcaj get dup dcai get exch dcai 1 add get 1 index sub dcau mul add
    dcGrid dcaj 1 add get dup dcai get exch dcai 1 add get 1 index sub dcau mul add
    1 index sub dcav mul add
} bind def

% x y r -   (a speck as a subpath)
% @internal
/dcgdot {
    /dcgr exch def /dcgy exch def /dcgx exch def
    dcgx dcgr add dcgy moveto dcgx dcgy dcgr 0 360 arc closepath
} bind def

% Grain specks offered per square point of board, at /Haze 0.7.
% @internal
/dcgrainK 0.09 def

% -   (loose dust specks: scattered along the ledge band, thinning upward)
% @internal
/dcspecks {
    gsave
        /.setfilladjust2 where { pop 0 0 .setfilladjust2 } if
        dcBx dcBy translate
        dcBw dcLedge mul 0.35 mul cvi {
            /dcpx dcrand dcBw mul def
            /dcpy dcrand dup mul dcLedge mul 1.5 mul def
            dcpy dcBh lt {
                dcpx dcpy dcfieldat dcrand 0.6 mul 0.35 add add 1 dcmin dcfcolor setrgbcolor
                newpath dcpx dcpy dcrand 0.25 mul 0.12 add 0 360 arc fill
            } if
        } repeat
    grestore
} bind def

% -   (re-lays the film over every other swipe's band: the eraser took
% the ghost writing there and left only its dust)
% @internal
/dcreswipe {
    0 2 dcSw length 1 sub {
        dcSw exch get 0 6 getinterval aload pop /dcsb exch def /dcsa1 exch def /dcsa0 exch def
        /dcsr exch def /dcscy exch def /dcscx exch def
        gsave
            dcBx dcBy translate
            newpath
            dcscx dcscy dcsr dcsb 0.7 mul add dcsa0 dcsa1 arc
            dcscx dcscy dcsr dcsb 0.7 mul sub 0.1 dcmax dcsa1 dcsa0 arcn
            closepath clip
            dcBx neg dcBy neg translate
            dcpaintfield
        grestore
    } for
} bind def

% -   (re-lays the field inside every wipe, cutting the ghost writing the
% wipe passed over; the field is already darker there)
% @internal
/dcrewipe {
    dcWp {
        0 6 getinterval aload pop pop /dcma exch def /dcmh exch def /dcml exch def
        /dcmy exch def /dcmx exch def
        gsave
            dcBx dcBy translate
            newpath
            gsave
                dcmx dcmy translate dcma rotate dcml dcmh 0.97 mul scale
                0 0 1 0 360 arc closepath
            grestore
            clip
            dcBx neg dcBy neg translate
            dcpaintfield
        grestore
    } forall
} bind def

% -   (the chalk tray under the board: a lip, and a floor covered in
% fallen dust, thickest against the board)
% @internal
/dctray {
    gsave
        /.setfilladjust2 where { pop 0 0 .setfilladjust2 } if
        % the floor, below the board, dusted: a smooth ramp from heavy dust
        % at the back (the strip nearest the board is the dustiest)
        0 1 7 {
            8 div /dctk exch def
            dcG [0.74 0.74 0.71] 0.85 dctk 0.5 mul sub dclerp3 aload pop setrgbcolor
            dcBx dcBy dcTray dctk 0.125 add mul sub dcBw dcTray 8 div 0.01 add rectfill
        } for
        % the lip: a lighter edge catching the light
        [0.80 0.80 0.77] aload pop setrgbcolor
        dcBx dcBy dcTray sub dcBw dcTray 0.18 mul rectfill
        % crumbs on the floor
        dcBw 0.15 mul cvi {
            dcG [0.62 0.62 0.60] dcrand 0.3 mul 0.55 add dclerp3 aload pop setrgbcolor
            newpath
            dcBx dcrand dcBw mul add
            dcBy dcTray dcrand dup mul 0.8 mul 0.15 add mul sub
            dcrand 0.35 mul 0.1 add 0 360 arc fill
        } repeat
    grestore
} bind def

% x y w h opts -   (validates dcboard's keys into dc* globals)
% @internal
/dcboardopts {
    /dcO exch def
    /dcO load type /dicttype ne { dcboard-options-must-be-a-dict } if
    4 { dup dcisnum not { dcboard-box-must-be-four-numbers } if 4 1 roll } repeat
    /dcBh exch def /dcBw exch def /dcBy exch def /dcBx exch def
    dcBw 0 le dcBh 0 le or { dcboard-box-must-have-positive-size } if
    /dcBn dcO /Surface /greenboard dcget def
    /dcBn load type /nametype ne { dcboard-surface-must-be-a-name } if
    dcboards /dcBn load known not { dcboard-surface-must-be-slate-or-greenboard } if
    /dcG dcO /Ground dcboards /dcBn load get /Ground get dcget
        /dcboard-ground-must-be-an-rgb-array dcrgb def
    /dcBseed dcO /Seed 1 -2147483646 2147483646 /dcboard-seed-must-be-a-number dcoptnum cvi def
    /dcWeave dcO /Weave 0.7 1e-3 100 /dcboard-weave-must-be-positive dcoptnum def
    /dcHaze dcO /Haze 0.4 0 1 /dcboard-haze-must-be-0-to-1 dcoptnum def
    /dchk dchazeq dcHaze dup mul mul dchazek add def
    /dcErased dcO /Erased 0.8 0 1 /dcboard-erased-must-be-0-to-1 dcoptnum def
    /dcNsw dcO /Swipes 6 0 40 /dcboard-swipes-must-be-0-to-40 dcoptnum cvi def
    /dcNwp dcO /Wipes 2 0 20 /dcboard-wipes-must-be-0-to-20 dcoptnum cvi def
    /dcNsm dcO /Smudges 3 0 20 /dcboard-smudges-must-be-0-to-20 dcoptnum cvi def
    /dcLedge dcO /Ledge 18 0 1e6 /dcboard-ledge-must-not-be-negative dcoptnum def
    /dcTray dcO /Tray 0 0 1e6 /dcboard-tray-must-not-be-negative dcoptnum def
    dcO /Ghost known {
        /dcGhost dcO /Ghost get def
        /dcGhost load dcisproc not { dcboard-ghost-must-be-a-procedure } if
        /dcGhostOn true def
    } { /dcGhostOn false def } ifelse
    /dcWhite dcchalks /white get def
} bind def

% -   (lays out the swipes, smudges and wipes from the board's seed)
% @internal
/dcboardlayout {
    dcBseed dcsrand dcrand pop
    /dcP [ 0 1 255 { } for ] def
    255 -1 1 {
        /dcpi exch def
        /dcpj dcrand dcpi 1 add mul floor cvi dcpi dcmin def
        dcP dcpi get dcP dcpj get dcP exch dcpi exch put dcP exch dcpj exch put
    } for
    % noise scales: a cloud about a third of the board, a finer one a ninth
    /dcCs1 3 dcBw dcBh dcmin div def
    /dcCs2 9 dcBw dcBh dcmin div def
    % swipes: [cx cy r a0 a1 halfwidth], arcs of an eraser swung from the
    % wrist, about one eraser (a fifth of the board's height) wide
    /dcSw [
        dcNsw {
            /dcsr dcBh dcrand 0.5 mul 0.45 add mul def
            /dcsx dcrand dcBw mul def /dcsy dcrand 0.8 mul 0.15 add dcBh mul def
            /dcsa dcrand 360 mul def
            [ dcsx dcsa cos dcsr mul sub dcsy dcsa sin dcsr mul sub dcsr
              dcsa 25 sub dcrand 20 mul sub dcdeg
              dcsa 25 add dcrand 20 mul add dcdeg
              dcBh 0.075 mul dcrand 0.4 mul 0.8 add mul ]
        } repeat
    ] def
    % smudges and wipes: [cx cy halflength halfheight angle weight]
    /dcSm [
        dcNsm {
            [ dcrand dcBw mul dcrand dcBh mul
              dcBh dcrand 0.12 mul 0.08 add mul dcBh dcrand 0.03 mul 0.025 add mul
              dcrand 50 mul 25 sub dcrand 0.25 mul 0.2 add dcHaze 0.3 add mul ]
        } repeat
    ] def
    /dcWp [
        dcNwp {
            [ dcrand dcBw mul dcrand 0.7 mul 0.2 add dcBh mul
              dcBh dcrand 0.1 mul 0.07 add mul dcBh dcrand 0.025 mul 0.025 add mul
              dcrand 40 mul 20 sub 0 ]
        } repeat
    ] def
    % each feature's bounding box, so the field skips it cheaply
    /dcSw [ dcSw { dcswbox } forall ] def
    /dcSm [ dcSm { dcellbox } forall ] def
    /dcWp [ dcWp { dcellbox } forall ] def
} bind def

% [cx cy r a0 a1 b] -> [cx cy r a0 a1 b x0 y0 x1 y1]   (the swipe's box,
% from 17 points along its arc, grown by the band's half-width)
% @internal
/dcswbox {
    /dcxa exch def
    /dcx0 1e9 def /dcy0 1e9 def /dcx1 -1e9 def /dcy1 -1e9 def
    /dcxs dcxa 4 get dcxa 3 get sub dcdeg def
    0 1 16 {
        16 div dcxs mul dcxa 3 get add /dcxt exch def
        dcxa 0 get dcxt cos dcxa 2 get mul add
        dcxa 1 get dcxt sin dcxa 2 get mul add
        dup dcy0 lt { dup /dcy0 exch def } if dup dcy1 gt { dup /dcy1 exch def } if pop
        dup dcx0 lt { dup /dcx0 exch def } if dup dcx1 gt { dup /dcx1 exch def } if pop
    } for
    /dcxb dcxa 5 get dcxa 2 get 0.02 mul add def
    [ dcxa aload pop dcx0 dcxb sub dcy0 dcxb sub dcx1 dcxb add dcy1 dcxb add ]
} bind def

% [cx cy l h a w] -> [cx cy l h a w x0 y0 x1 y1]   (an ellipse's box)
% @internal
/dcellbox {
    /dcxa exch def
    /dcxb dcxa 2 get dcxa 3 get dcmax def
    [ dcxa aload pop dcxa 0 get dcxb sub dcxa 1 get dcxb sub
      dcxa 0 get dcxb add dcxa 1 get dcxb add ]
} bind def

% feature -> bool   (the field point dcfx dcfy lies in its box)
% @internal
/dcinbox {
    dup 6 get dcfx le 1 index 8 get dcfx ge and
    1 index 7 get dcfy le and exch 9 get dcfy ge and
} bind def

% x y w h opts -   (dcboard's body, run inside dcscratch)
% @internal
/dcboardbody {
    userdict /dkBatch known { dcboard-cannot-run-inside-a-dkbatch } if
    dcboardopts
    << /Surface dcBn /Seed dcBseed /Ground dcG /Weave dcWeave >> dksurface
    dcboardlayout
    dcbuildfield
    % Every gsave below is given back before an error propagates (drykit's
    % dkZdraw pattern), so a failed board leaves no clip behind.
    gsave
        newpath dcBx dcBy dcBw dcBh rectclip
        {
            % the board, then its film
            dcG aload pop setrgbcolor dcBx dcBy dcBw dcBh rectfill
            dcpaintfield
            userdict /dcUnder [ dcFmean dcfcolor ] put
            userdict /dcUnderG userdict /dkGround get put
            dcBseed 5 mul 1 add dcsrand dcrand pop
            dcgrain
            % the erased writing, then half the swipes lay the film back
            dcGhostOn { dcghost dcreswipe } if
            dcBseed 7 mul 3 add dcsrand dcrand pop
            dcstreaks
            dcrewipe
            dcLedge 0 gt { dcspecks } if
        } stopped
    grestore
    { stop } if
    dcTray 0 gt { dctray } if
} bind def

% -   (the /Ghost writing: the caller's path, drawn as what the eraser
% left in the tooth, one faint and broken level of chalk)
% @internal
/dcghost {
    gsave
        {
            newpath
            count /dcgc exch def
            % the caller's procedure runs in the caller's dictionaries
            /dcGhost load end stopped dcscratch begin { stop } if
            count dcgc gt { count dcgc sub { pop } repeat dcboard-ghost-must-leave-the-stack-alone } if
            count dcgc lt { dcboard-ghost-must-leave-the-stack-alone } if
            dcWhite aload pop setrgbcolor
            dcscratch /dcLevels 1 put
            << /Width dcGhostW /Touch dcGhostT /Strength 1 dcErased sub 0.25 mul 0.03 add
               /Dust 0 /Landing 0.2 /Lift 0.2 /Vary 1 /Streak 2.6
               /Seed dcBseed 31 mul 2147483 mod >> dcchalk
        } stopped
        % the one-level dial is the ghost's alone, error or not
        dcscratch /dcLevels undef
    grestore
    { stop } if
} bind def

%%SelfTest: dcboard-rejects-malformed-options
%   { 0 0 100 100 5 dcboard } /dcboard-options-must-be-a-dict (a number for the options) mustguard
%   { 0 0 (w) 100 << >> dcboard } /dcboard-box-must-be-four-numbers (a string width) mustguard
%   { 0 0 0 100 << >> dcboard } /dcboard-box-must-have-positive-size (a zero width) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Surface /marble >> dcboard } /dcboard-surface-must-be-slate-or-greenboard (/Surface /marble) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Surface (slate) >> dcboard } /dcboard-surface-must-be-a-name (a string /Surface) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Ground [0 0] >> dcboard } /dcboard-ground-must-be-an-rgb-array (a two-entry /Ground) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Seed (s) >> dcboard } /dcboard-seed-must-be-a-number (a string /Seed) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Haze 2 >> dcboard } /dcboard-haze-must-be-0-to-1 (/Haze 2) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Erased -1 >> dcboard } /dcboard-erased-must-be-0-to-1 (/Erased -1) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Swipes 99 >> dcboard } /dcboard-swipes-must-be-0-to-40 (/Swipes 99) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Wipes -1 >> dcboard } /dcboard-wipes-must-be-0-to-20 (/Wipes -1) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Smudges 21 >> dcboard } /dcboard-smudges-must-be-0-to-20 (/Smudges 21) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Ledge -3 >> dcboard } /dcboard-ledge-must-not-be-negative (/Ledge -3) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Tray -1 >> dcboard } /dcboard-tray-must-not-be-negative (/Tray -1) mustguard
%   { { 0 0 50 40 << >> dcboard } dkbatch } /dcboard-cannot-run-inside-a-dkbatch (dcboard in a batch) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Ghost 3 >> dcboard } /dcboard-ghost-must-be-a-procedure (a number /Ghost) mustguard
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Ghost { 1 } >> dcboard } /dcboard-ghost-must-leave-the-stack-alone
%   (a /Ghost that leaves a value) mustguard
%   gsave { 0 0 50 40 << /Ghost { pop } >> dcboard } stopped grestore
%   (a /Ghost that underflows raises) mustbe
%   count 0 eq (and leaves the operand stack empty) mustbe
%   { gsave 0 0 50 40 << >> dcboard grestore } (the defaults) mustpass
%   { gsave 0 0 50 40 << /Surface /slate /Ghost { 5 20 moveto 45 22 lineto } >> dcboard grestore }
%   (a slate with a ghost) mustpass
%   true setpacking /dcpk { 5 20 moveto 45 22 lineto } def false setpacking
%   { gsave 0 0 50 40 << /Ghost /dcpk load >> dcboard grestore } (a packed /Ghost) mustpass
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: dcboard-an-error-mid-board-leaves-no-clip
%   % A ghost too wide for drykit's catch budget raises cleanly: the board's
%   % clip and gsaves are given back, and the ghost's one-level dial does
%   % not leak into later chalk.
%   /dkbudget 20 def
%   { 0 0 50 40 << /Ghost { 5 20 moveto 45 22 lineto } >> dcboard }
%   /dkcatch-area-exceeds-safety-limit (a ghost past the catch budget) mustguard
%   /dkbudget 150000 def
%   dcscratch /dcLevels known not (the ghost's dial is gone) mustbe
%   clippath pathbbox 4 2 roll pop pop 60 gt exch 60 gt and
%   (no board clip is left in force) mustbe
%   gsave { 0 0 50 40 << /Ghost { 1 2 } >> dcboard } stopped grestore
%   (a /Ghost that leaves values raises) mustbe
%   count 0 eq (and its values are popped) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
%%SelfTest: dcboard-takes-no-draws-and-sets-the-board
%   1 srand rand /sttaken exch def
%   1 srand
%   gsave 0 0 80 60 << /Surface /slate /Ghost { 5 30 moveto 70 34 lineto } /Swipes 3 >> dcboard grestore
%   rand sttaken eq (dcboard leaves the caller's stream untouched) mustbe
%   userdict /dkSurface get /slate eq (dcboard sets the drykit surface) mustbe
%   userdict /dcUnder known (and the tone chalk is drawn against) mustbe
%   << /Width 2 >> dcchalkopts dcUnderC userdict /dcUnder get eq
%   (chalk on that board blends against the board's tone) mustbe
%   << /Surface /drawing >> dksurface
%   << /Width 2 >> dcchalkopts dcUnderC userdict /dkGround get eq
%   (after a new surface, chalk blends against that surface's ground) mustbe
%%EndSelfTest
%
% @kind: Procedure
% @summary: Dresses a chalkboard in the rectangle x y w h and makes it the surface for dcchalk: the board's tone (black slate or green board), a dust film that is never flat, faint writing left from an earlier lecture and eraser swipe arcs over it, darker finger wipes that cut through the old writing, lighter smudges, and dust settled thick along the bottom ledge. Calls dksurface; takes no rand draws from the caller's stream.
% @example: (lib/handscript.ps) run (lib/strokeglyphs.ps) run 40 40 520 300 << /Surface /slate /Ghost { (old notes) 60 200 << /Size 30 >> sgpath } >> dcboard
% @param: /Surface [name] /slate (near-black) or /greenboard (dark green) (default /greenboard)
% @param: /Ground [rgb array] The bare board's tone, under the dust (default from /Surface)
% @param: /Weave [points] The board's tooth spacing, passed to dksurface: coarser than drykit's own board surfaces, because at 2x a 0.4pt tooth is below a pixel and costs twice the cells (default 0.7)
% @param: /Seed [integer] Seed for the board's tooth, haze, swipes, wipes and specks; leaves the caller's rand stream untouched (default 1)
% @param: /Haze [0..1] How thick the film of chalk dust over the board is; 0 is a freshly washed board (default 0.4)
% @param: /Ghost [proc] Earlier writing, as a procedure that builds a path and paints nothing (sgpath calls, lines), which dcboard draws as chalk that was erased; it must leave the operand stack as it found it (default none)
% @param: /Erased [0..1] How thoroughly the ghost writing was erased: 1 leaves almost nothing (default 0.8)
% @param: /Swipes [count] Eraser swipe arcs across the board, 0..40 (default 6)
% @param: /Wipes [count] Finger or cloth wipes, darker than the haze because they took the dust off, 0..20 (default 2)
% @param: /Smudges [count] Lighter smears of dust from a hand or sleeve, 0..20 (default 3)
% @param: /Ledge [points] Height of the band along the bottom edge where fallen dust settles; 0 for none (default 18)
% @param: /Tray [points] Depth of the chalk tray drawn below the board, its floor grey with fallen dust; 0 draws none (default 0)
/dcboard {
    dcscratch begin
    { dcboardbody } stopped
    end
    { stop } if
} bind def
